up until about half a year ago Anna was  still living with a self-proclaimed  Islamic state in Syria and although  she's now back home in Kosovo she  insists on wearing her niqab or full  body veil while ninety percent of the  population in Kosovo are Sunni Muslim  few women wear the niqab most people  here practise a more liberal form of  Islam any time I go outside they look at  us like a UFO aliens I can see it's  bizarre for them to see us like this but  for me it's not a problem  Anna stayed until is lost its last  holdout bagus in a military strike  approved by federal authorities we meet  her at a lake outside Kosovo's capital  Pristina like all female attorneys she  lives under house arrest awaiting trial  Anna as the first Kosovar attorney to  speak on camera about her experience we  wanted to know what she thought about  the beheadings and other reported crimes  against humanity committed in the name  of the Islamic state but Ana avoided our  questions and focus instead on her own  suffering but the airstrikes when they  came they just bombed a house you know a  big building there were kids there there  were women mothers everything at the  Pristina Airport one night in April  Europe's youngest country and one of its  poorest brought 32 women seventy four  children and four male fighters back  home all of them had been imprisoned in  Kurdish camps in northern Syria after  the military defeat of the Islamic state  the operation was a strategic move for  the cause of our government based on the  belief that back home under official  observation the returnees would pose  less of a security risk than if they  remained abroad the aim is to  reintegrate all of them into society  including the men who were sent to  prison right away meanwhile the  authorities are closely monitoring the  women and children and offering them  counseling they didn't see any  resistance  from them to be part of our programs and  maybe this also is due that due to the  fact that they know that it was Kosovo  authorities who took the decision to  repatriate them in Kosovo otherwise they  would have remained in the conflict zone  the cause of our approach is unique no  other European government has undertaken  similar steps rich Western democracies  such as Germany France and Britain have  so far rejected any organized return of  their citizens who joined the  self-proclaimed Islamic state the fear  of not being able to control their  attorneys appears to be too great Ana  tells us she didn't know what she was  getting herself into when she followed  her husband to Syria in 2014 I was going  to go somewhere else with my husband I  didn't know I was going there my husband  prepared everything for me to go there  but didn't tell me suddenly I found  myself in Syria after her husband died  in battle she married another fighter  whose fate remains unclear early this  year after the military defeat of the  Islamic state Ana surrendered like  thousands of others she ended up in the  alcohol refugee camp a living hell she  says and I cannot understand why other  European countries are refusing to take  their citizens back we are human we are  people if someone makes mistake one time  we shouldn't just leave them like that  the women they are so sensitive the kids  what is their fault you know so we  should help them  Friday prayer in a mosque in central  Pristina after the ethnic wars of the  1990s religious organizations and  charities from Saudi Arabia and other  Gulf states had a growing influence in  Kosovo with them came a much more  conservative school of Islam one that  taught the supremacy of the Sharia law  and ideas of violent jihad more than  four hundred young Kosovars joined  extremist groups in Syria and Iraq  between 2012 and 2017 the highest rate  per head in Europe the authorities are  convinced that the Saudi influence was  the driving force behind their  radicalization I believe that the idea  of the NGOs and extremists that brought  ideology here was to change the very  cold value of the society to introduce  political Islam in Kosovo and also to  establish a network that one day they  may become also they may have also  political power and become a political  factor the Imam of this mosque in  central Pristina which itself is  considered to be a rather conservative  points to other reasons than just fara  communities in our country many young  people didn't have any future prospects  over the last twenty years  the main reasons for this the poor  economic situation a social state of the  country and the lack of prospects in  general but we should not deny the role  YouTube that's played in radicalization  I would like to point out that these  people have not been radicalized by  their religious representatives visitor  to life at all we are on our way to  another of the 32 female attorneys Laura  agreed to an interview only on the  condition of total anonymity we are not  allowed to show where and how she lives  her first two husbands are dead the  third imprisoned in a camp like Ana  Laura spent several years in Syria I was  very young I didn't think I just  followed my husband I expected something  totally different in life that is why I  feel betrayed it was a mistake  war is not a place for women and  children war is bad for everyone I would  never go there again so far Laura  doesn't have any concrete plans for the  future just hopes I want to live as a  free person integrated into society I  hope that the people will accept me as I  am  even with my hijab Laura Anna and the  other women psychiatrist fabu not Ofili  knows them all she is in charge of the  government's rehabilitation and  reintegration program for the female  returnees and their children due to  doctor-patient confidentiality Tafel I  won't say whether she thinks some of the  women might possibly be dangerous she  and her team talked to the returnees on  a regular basis in the clinic and in  their homes all the women and children  who returned from Syria were traumatized  they came back from a war zone where  they witnessed cruel crimes and heavy  bombing which has led to trauma and  post-traumatic stress disorder PTSD  there have been a number of activities  specially designed to help the children  cope with what they have been through  young innocent victims of a war their  parents dragged them into back in a safe  environment they can finally play like  other children the older kids just  started regular school after the summer  holidays it is too early to talk about  greater improvements but we are seeing  some progress back to normal life in  Kosovo just in Kosovo  catch anak about 50 kilometers south of  Pristina the small town of about 10,000  inhabitants at the border to Macedonia  was considered one of the hot beds of  radicalization about two dozen people  are said to have left for the war zones  in Syria and Iraq from kitronik  in the surrounding area alone on the  streets people seem supportive of taking  them back don't need any gap in the city  they have made mistakes and we have to  help them reintegrate into our society  these are our people it was a good  decision to bring them back I support  this initiative these people shouldn't  have gone to Syria we are in Kosovo on  the Balkans  we don't need Wars we have been through  war ourselves I don't think these people  have come back to do harm here like they  did there that's why we shouldn't  exclude them Anna wants to leave the  past behind she says she feels accepted  by her family and neighborhood no one  talks to her about her time in Syria and  are still making sense of why she's  under house arrest we came from a place  of war it is very normal they are  investigating us but in the end they  will understand that we aren't at fault  we didn't do anything  maybe our mistake is that we were there  we lived there Anna is still convinced  that she didn't do anything wrong but  ultimately that's up to Kosovo's  authorities to decide
 

